<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos Antonio López</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1792–1862</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos Antonio López was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> from 1841 until his death in 1862, first as consul and from 1844 as president. Ending the isolation imposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gaspar-rodriguez-de-francia">José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia</a>, he opened the country to trade, hired foreign engineers and built an ironworks, a shipyard and one of the first railways in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-solano-lopez">Francisco Solano López</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-antonio-lopez.jpg" alt="Carlos Antonio López" width="296" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_Antonio_L%C3%B3pez_retrato_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
